"""Scan Orca's shipped code for calls a game fix must never add, and report the ones that are new.

  Tools/orca/forbidden-calls                  scan the working tree
  Tools/orca/forbidden-calls --rev B          scan the tree at revision B
  Tools/orca/forbidden-calls --rev B --since A
                                              also mark which hit lines B added since it left A
                                              (from their merge-base), and report hits on lines a
                                              change added in game logic even when no count rose
  Tools/orca/forbidden-calls --baseline-rev origin/main --rev B
                                              gate a branch against the stable branch's baseline,
                                              so a branch can't approve its own hits
  Tools/orca/forbidden-calls --update-baseline
                                              accept today's hits (a person reviews that diff)
  --list   print every hit, not just new ones;  --json   machine-readable

What is scanned: Orca's own code (every %orca-root in layers.txt plus every file that differs from
upstream Dolphin), C/C++/Objective-C (.inc/.ipp too) and CMake files, minus paths layers.txt marks
`dev` (tests and tools never ship); in game-logic paths every text file. Comments are ignored;
string literals are kept; backslash-continued lines are joined.

Rules:
  socket        BSD/Winsock calls and headers, ENet, libjuice, SFML network, asio, mbedtls_net
  http          curl, Dolphin's HttpRequest and WebSocket, NSURLSession, WinHTTP, URL literals
  file-open     opening, writing, renaming or deleting files. In game logic, every one; elsewhere,
                on a line that doesn't build its path from the Dolphin user or Sys directory
                (GetUserPath, GetSysDirectory, D_*_IDX), or that has a `..` path segment
  file-user     the same on a line that builds its path from the user or Sys directory (not in game
                logic, where they are file-open): the user directory holds Config, GameSettings,
                the NAND and Load/Textures
  process       system, popen, fork, exec*, posix_spawn, CreateProcess, ShellExecute, NSTask
  dynload       dlopen, dlsym, LoadLibrary, GetProcAddress, NSBundle, DynamicLibrary
  env           getenv and friends, environ, Orca's env helpers (GetEnv, Env, EnvIs, EnvInt...)
                (in game logic, process, dynload, env and fopen also match as bare names: a
                function pointer, `(std::getenv)(...)`, a #define)
  app-channel   the app protocol: Orca::Status calls (with their first string argument: a new
                `orca ...` line or state is a new key) and every stdout/stderr write (printf, puts,
                std::cout/cerr, fmt::print, stdout/stderr, write(1|2, ...), GetStdHandle)
  include-escape  an #include whose path has a `..` segment
  peer-parse    decoding primitives (memcpy, reinterpret_cast, picojson, Parse*/Decode*, strto*,
                from_chars, swaps) in code that reads bytes from peers, the room, the app or a game
                bundle (PEER_FILES: all engine-sensitive, test_layers.py checks)
  determinism   game logic only: threads, wall clocks, rand and random devices, Common::Timer
  settings      game logic only: IniFile load/save, Config::Set*, savestate save/load
  build-deps    find_package, add_subdirectory, FetchContent, pkg-config, execute_process,
                add_custom_command/target, file(DOWNLOAD|UPLOAD), include(), target_sources,
                try_run, -include / /FI in compile options, and each library given to
                target_link_libraries in CMake files

A hit is keyed by (rule, path, token), where the token is the call or header plus its string
argument when it has one (getenv("ORCA_X"), a URL). The baseline holds how many of each key are
allowed. A key whose count goes up is a NEW hit, so moving or editing a line is quiet, while a
new call, a new environment variable, a new URL or a new library is reported. A file with no
baseline entries (Orca's first change to an upstream file) is allowed what upstream Dolphin's copy
of it has, so only Orca's additions count. With --since, a hit in game logic on a line the change
added is reported even when its count didn't rise (an edit to a baselined line).
As a gate, run the stable branch's copy against the branch (the running tools must be the rev's
named by --rules-rev / --baseline-rev, or it refuses):
  <main checkout>/Tools/orca/forbidden-calls --repo <branch worktree> --rules-rev origin/main \
      --baseline-rev origin/main --rev <branch> --since origin/main
Exit status: 0 clean, 1 new or edited hits, 2 error.
"""

